"""Full Rectangular Boxes size table from Zero Manufacturing's Deep Drawn
catalog.

Transcribed 2026-09-18 from a customer-provided PDF (Deep-Draw-Catalog-8-
6-2026-Update-1.pdf), "Standard Cans and Boxes" / Rectangular Boxes size
table (catalog pages 8-29, PDF pages 10-31). This is a scanned/image PDF
with no extractable text layer and no OCR available in this environment
(no `tesseract`, no sudo to install it), so every row below was read
directly off a rendered page image (PyMuPDF, no poppler needed) rather
than OCR'd or estimated.

Not every row on those pages made it in: most rows in the 0.3"-2" width
range say "See page 56/57/58" and point to separate mini-series tables
(ZMR, ZMS, etc.) with a different schema entirely -- coded material
thickness (A/B/C) instead of a direct gauge, a single combined corner
radius instead of separate R1/R2, and several possible alloys per code
(steel/aluminum/brass/Monel). Per 2026-09-17 scoping with Alan, those
cross-referenced mini-series are out of scope for this pass; only rows
with a direct part number, gauge, and R1/R2 inline on the main
Rectangular Boxes table are included here. That's still every such row
across all 22 pages (906 of them), not a hand-picked sample.

Almost every row uses 6061-0 aluminum, but it is NOT universal: ZT19-75
(catalog page 10) is 3003-0 -- confirmed by re-reading that row at 6x
zoom since it looked like an outlier. That's why alloy is a per-row
field instead of a single module-level constant (an earlier pass had
assumed "every row is 6061-0" before the full page-by-page pass turned
this row up).

See case_configurator/valan_catalog.py for VAL-AN, a separate, unrelated
catalog with its own 6061/1100-0 alloy mix. Per the user's instruction,
the two catalogs are kept as separate tools/agents, not merged.

Fields per box:
- part_no: the catalog's stocked or non-stocked part number.
- width_in, length_in: outside footprint dimensions (W x L), inches.
- height_min_in, height_max_in: the height (H) range achievable for this
  W x L, adjustable to any value in that range per the catalog.
- gauge_in: blank gauge thickness (T) prior to drawing, inches. Actual
  wall thickness runs ~75%+ of this depending on draw depth/count (see
  deep_draw_pricing.py for how that's modeled).
- alloy: aluminum alloy for this specific row (6061-0 for all but one row).
- r1_in, r2_in: bottom and side corner radii, inches.
- catalog_page: the printed page number in the source PDF, for traceability.
"""

